  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>

  <p>
    The skills needed for your first job in India split into two layers. One layer is role-specific: coding
    for an engineer, accounting basics for a finance role, design tools for a UI/UX role. The other layer is
    foundational, and it applies no matter which role you land: how clearly you communicate, how you handle
    email and workplace etiquette, how comfortable you are with a spreadsheet, and how well you manage your
    time and commitments. Most fresher prep focuses almost entirely on the first layer. The second layer is
    what actually gets watched most closely once you are hired, and it is often what gets checked during the
    interview itself, even for a technical role.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Nobody grades you on these skills in college, so it is easy to assume they will sort themselves out once
      you start working. They mostly do not sort themselves out on their own. A fresher who never had to write
      a professional email, prioritise competing deadlines, or explain a decision clearly out loud usually
      learns these skills the hard way, in front of the manager who is deciding how much to trust them.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="why-it-matters">Why these skills matter more than your degree right now</h2>

  <p>
    Overall graduate employability in India has been rising, reaching around 56% in the India Skills Report
    2026, and companies report plans to hire meaningfully more freshers in the coming year than the year
    before. That sounds like good news for freshers, and in one sense it is. But the same report also flags a
    persistent, specific gap: communication skills, problem-solving, teamwork, and critical thinking remain
    weak even among candidates who are technically competent, particularly among first-generation learners
    without prior exposure to a professional workplace. Around 80% of companies surveyed said they still
    struggle to find the right fresher candidates to fill open roles, despite a large pool of graduates.
  </p>

  <p>
    That gap is not primarily about degrees or coding ability. It is about the layer underneath: whether a
    fresher can explain themselves clearly, write a professional message, handle a spreadsheet without
    hand-holding, and manage a deadline without needing to be chased. Building those skills deliberately, before
    your first job rather than during it, is one of the more overlooked ways to close the gap employers keep
    naming.
  </p>

  <h2 id="skill-split">Technical skills vs foundational skills: what changes by role and industry</h2>

  <p>
    The technical skill half of your first job changes a lot by role. A software role needs a programming
    language and Git basics; a sales role needs a CRM and pitch structure; a finance role needs spreadsheet
    formulas and reconciliation habits. What barely changes is the foundational half: some form of clear
    communication and reliability shows up as the second column in every row below, no matter the industry.
  </p>

