  <h2 id="communication">Communication: the skill every other skill depends on</h2>

  <p>
    Communication shows up as the top employability gap in survey after survey, and it is rarely about
    vocabulary or accent. It is about structure: can you explain a problem, a decision, or a request in a way
    that someone with less context than you can follow without asking three clarifying questions.
  </p>

  <p>
    In practice, that means three separate abilities. Speaking clearly enough that a manager does not have to
    guess at your point. Writing clearly enough that an email or update does not need a follow-up call to
    decode. And listening well enough that you ask a sharp follow-up question instead of nodding and hoping it
    becomes clear later. All three are practicable on your own, well before you have a job to practise them in:
    explaining a college project to someone unfamiliar with it, writing a short summary of an article in three
    sentences, or recording yourself answering a common interview question and listening back for filler words
    and unclear structure.
  </p>

  <h2 id="email-etiquette">Email and workplace etiquette basics</h2>

  <p>
    Email is often the first sustained written impression you make on a manager or client, and it is one of
    the easiest places to make a small, visible mistake in your first week. Indian workplace communication
    tends to favour formal greetings, polite and structured messages, and clear written follow-ups after verbal
    conversations, so it is worth building the habit before you are relying on it under pressure.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Situation</th>
          <th>Common fresher mistake</th>
          <th>What actually works</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {emailRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Situation"><strong>{item.situation}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Common fresher mistake">{item.weak}</td>
