          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Beyond email, basic workplace etiquette also covers punctuality for meetings, coming prepared with your
    part of the discussion ready, and following up in writing on anything agreed to verbally, so nothing
    depends on memory alone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="excel-literacy">Basic Excel and spreadsheet literacy</h2>

  <p>
    Excel or a spreadsheet tool shows up in a large share of job postings across industries in India, not just
    in finance or data roles. MIS reporting, sales tracking, HR records, marketing performance sheets, and
    routine operations checklists all run on spreadsheets in most companies, which means a fresher who is not
    comfortable with one is quietly dependent on a colleague for tasks that should take a few minutes alone.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Bare minimum before day one</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Sorting and filtering a data set without breaking the formatting.</li>
        <li>Basic formulas: SUM, AVERAGE, COUNT, and simple IF statements.</li>
        <li>Formatting a sheet so someone else can read it without asking what a column means.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Worth learning early if you have time</div>
      <ul>
        <li>VLOOKUP or XLOOKUP to pull data from another sheet or table.</li>
        <li>A basic pivot table to summarise a data set without manual counting.</li>
        <li>Simple chart creation to turn a table into something a manager can scan in seconds.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    You do not need to be a data analyst to benefit from this. The goal is being self-sufficient with routine
    reporting tasks, not building financial models on day one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="digital-literacy">Digital and tool literacy employers expect beyond Excel</h2>

  <p>
    Spreadsheets get most of the attention, but everyday digital literacy is checked just as often, usually
    without anyone naming it directly. Most of this comes down to a handful of habits you can build before you
    ever log into a company laptop.
  </p>

