  <h2 id="time-management">Time management and prioritization</h2>

  <p>
    In college, most deadlines are fixed and known months in advance. In a job, priorities shift, multiple
    people can hand you work at once, and the skill that matters is not how many hours you put in, but whether
    you can tell the difference between what is urgent and what is important, give a realistic estimate
    instead of an optimistic one, and flag a likely delay early instead of going quiet until the deadline
    passes.
  </p>

  <p>
    A simple habit closes most of this gap: at the start of each day or task, write down what genuinely needs
    to happen today versus what can wait, and if something looks at risk of slipping, say so to the person
    waiting on it as soon as you notice, not after the deadline has already passed. Managers consistently rate
    that single habit, flagging risk early, as one of the clearest signs of someone they can hand more
    responsibility to.
  </p>

  <h2 id="technical-interviews">How these skills get checked even in technical interviews</h2>

  <p>
    A common assumption among freshers preparing for a technical or core-skill role is that the interview will
    be entirely about the technical content: code, case studies, or domain questions. In practice, most
    structured interviews also include behavioural questions, commonly built around the
    <strong>STAR method</strong> (Situation, Task, Action, Result), where the interviewer is not only checking
    whether you solved a problem, but how clearly you can walk them through the decision behind it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Even inside a purely technical round, communication is being evaluated indirectly. When you are asked to
    explain your own project or walk through your code, the interviewer is watching whether you can make
    something complex understandable to someone who was not there when you built it, which is a direct preview
    of how you would explain your work to a team once hired. A candidate who solves the technical problem but
    cannot explain their own reasoning clearly reads as a real hiring risk, not a minor gap, because that gap
    does not go away once the offer letter is signed.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      A useful way to prepare: after finishing any project or assignment, practise explaining it out loud in
      under two minutes to someone unfamiliar with it. If they can follow your explanation without stopping to
      ask "wait, what do you mean," your communication is doing its job. If not, that is the gap to close before
      the interview, not during it.
    </p>
  </div>

